questions.answer:
You have a lot more freedom in choosing and trying out classes and experimenting with new topics at BOKU, which is super nice, but also quite chaotic at first. Especially because there's not really a strict system (like the schedule places A-D at UCPH) for which weekdays and for how long the courses are, you'll have to check that out for each class. And that means that there'll probably be a lot of overlap between your classes, also your compulsory ones. But as this is the system, the professors also know that this is how it is, and will probably state what is the minimum expected attendance for their class in the first lecture. It also depends on class type (see
https://boku.ac.at/en/international/themen/international-students-coming-to-boku/a-good-start-plan-your-studies-at-boku/wie-plane-ich-mein-studium-an-der-boku-vor-der-ankunft/kurstypen
), and of course how much overlap you can handle yourself. But it is almost impossible to get around having a bit of class overlap, I think – make sure that the overlapping classes are at the same campus then, when you have to run between them!
